## ABHA Number Service

The ABHA Number will be used for the purposes of uniquely identifying persons, authenticating them, and threading their health records (only with the informed consent of the patient) across multiple systems and stakeholders. The ABDM Sandbox is ready to be integrated with Hospital and Lab software such as EMR, HIMS, LIMS, Telemedicine, among others through the ABDM APIs. All the services on the sandbox are designed to work with the Healthidsbx environment. The ABHA Number created in the sandbox environment would have the suffix @sbx and you can create a sandbox ABHA Number by using the link healthidsbx.abdm.gov.in

Link to ABHA Document

As per the Health Information Provider (HIP) guidelines, healthcare providers need to do the following with ABHA Numbers:

#### 1. Collect ABHA Number During Registration

As ABHA Numbers start to get widely adopted, HIPs must check with Patients if they have a ABHA Number. If they do, then the HIPs must capture and validate ABHA Numbers at the point of patient registration. The process to be followed for correct capture and verification of ABHA Numbers is outlined in sections below. HIPs understand that this process is voluntary, and they will not force any patient to provide a ABHA Number if they do not want to share it.

#### 2. Issue ABHA Numbers To Interested Patients

HIPs are expected to educate patients and help create ABHA Numbers for those that require assistance. Several sections of society, which include the elderly population, illiterate users, etc. will require assistance in creation of ABHA Number. Aadhaar linked ABHA Numbers can be created for those that do not have a mobile phone. It is recommended that a printed ABHA Number card is provided to these patients to ensure they can use this card across multiple health institutions. If a Hospital is already issuing a patient registration card, it is encouraged to include the ABHA Number No and the ABHA Number QR code on its existing cards. This integration can be achieved using the ABHA Number Open APIs.

ABHA Numbers can be created by either self-registration or in an assisted manner at a healthcare provider or other authorized entities. The various mechanisms for issuing ABHA Numbers to interested users are provided below:

#### 1. Self Registration

ABHA Numbers can be created by providing a minimum set of information which include Full Name, Year of Birth, Gender and either a Mobile or an Aadhaar number. Most patients/ persons with smartphones are expected to self-enroll for the creation of a ABHA Number by downloading a PHR (Personal Health Record) mobile application or on the official ABHA Number portal (healthid.abdm.gov.in). Once a healthcare provider becomes a HIP, every patient, who registers with the HIP, with a mobile number must be sent an SMS with a deep link that will allow them to download a ABDM compatible PHR mobile application. These mobile applications must have the facility to create a ABHA Number during the signup process. Patients must be encouraged to create only one ABHA Number. This will help bring all their health records into a single longitudinal view. Patients who self-register can select an easy to remember ABHA Number of their choice like ashokb@abdm.

#### 2. Assisted Registration

Healthcare providers can assist patients in creating ABHA Numbers. This is especially required in the case of the elderly, digitally illiterate and those without mobile phones. Every ABHA Number must be created only after educating the patient regarding the benefits of the ABHA Number and obtaining a clear consent of the patient. Healthcare facilities can either create the ABHA Number for patients from the official ABHA Number portal (healthid.abdm.gov.in) or by integrating with the ABHA Number Open APIs from their own software. ABHA Numbers can be created with Aadhaar. This is recommended for patients who do not have smart phones or are digitally illiterate. An Aadhaar linked ABHA Number can only be created after a successful Aadhaar eKYC which requires a biometric or OTP based authentication. Healthcare providers must setup a desk with an Aadhaar certified biometric device to capture either fingerprint or iris biometrics. UIDAI publishes the list of certified devices on its website. See here. All patients obtaining ABHA Numbers via an assisted mode must also receive a printed physical card containing the ABHA Number and the QR code from the ABHA Number service. The ABHA Number service provides a standard card format that can be printed, folded and put into a plastic pouch or laminated. Users must be educated and encouraged to produce their ABHA Number at every Healthcare provider.

#### 3. ABHA Number And Existing Health Provider Cards

Many health providers already issue patient identifier cards containing a provider specific patient id. These providers are encouraged to update their processes to include the ABHA Number No and the ABHA Number QR code on their existing cards. This can be achieved by integrating with the ABHA Number Open APIs in their software.

You can check the API sequence to create ABHA Number and address here.

Furthermore, all HIPs are expected to follow a recommended process for capturing and verifying ABHA Numbers during patient registration. ABHA Number is designed to lower patient registration time and to help the healthcare provider in accurately identifying the patient, retrieving patient medical history prior to treatment and linking any new health records created for the patient.

All QR codes described below are standardized by ABDM for interoperability across the ecosystem.

#### 1. Provider Scans The ABHA Number QR Code

All patients who already have a ABHA Number can show the ABHA Number QR code in their PHR mobile application or a physical print out of their ABHA Number card. HIPs can use a QR code scanner to rapidly scan the code. The QR code contains the ABHA Number, ABHA Number (user selected), name, date of birth, gender, mobile number and address of the patient. The information is also digitally signed by ABDM and can be verified offline as well.

#### 2. User Scans A HIP QR Code

The health provider can paste a HIP QR code on their registration desk. Patients with PHR mobile applications can simply scan that code and consent to sharing their ABHA Number with that provider. The ABHA Number details are posted to the HIP service. The software used by the registration desk can retrieve the scanned ABHA Number and rapidly register the patient. The HIP will get all the details in the ABHA Number including ABHA Number, ABHA Number (user selected), name, date of birth, gender, mobile number and address of the patient.

#### 3. Patient Verbally Shares ABHA Number or ABHA Number

If the patient shares their ABHA Number or ABHA Number number verbally during registration, the software/ application is expected to check if this is a valid ABHA Number via an Open API. The API confirms if the ABHA Number is valid and also the authentication methods supported for the HealthID. The registration desk can choose an appropriate method to authenticate the user. The following Authentication methods are supported:

a. Mobile-OTP – An OTP is sent to the patient’s mobile number that is linked with the ABHA Number. The patient must share the OTP with the registration desk for verification

b. Mobile-IVR – Patient will receive a call on the mobile number linked with the ABHA Number. Patient confirms acceptance by pressing a key after listening to the IVR message

c. Mobile-App - Notification – A request to confirm the ABHA Number can be provided to the care provider will pop up in the patient’s ABHA Mobile Application. The patient can consent in the application to share the ABHA Number.

d. Aadhaar-OTP – A OTP is triggered with the Aadhaar linked mobile number of the Patient. The patient must provide the OTP to the registration desk to complete the authentication

e. Aadhaar-Biometric – Healthcare provider can perform a biometric auth using either fingerprint or iris to authenticate the patient’s ABHA Number.

On successful verification, the Healthcare provider will be able to obtain the details of the ABHA Number including name, date of birth, gender, mobile number and address of the patient.

