## FHR Framework Flows

This section helps you understand the various data flows within the FHR Framework, represented by detailed flow diagrams.

### Registering As a Patient Using The Patient App

The following sections explain how a patient can register via consent manager app. Once you've registered as a patient, you will be able to search hospitals and choose to link your patient care contexts for the selected one. This will enable access to medical records for a HIU or for the patient herself.

#### Mobile Number Verification

To register via the patient app, the first step is to register using a digitally verifiable identifier. As of now, Aadhaar and mobile numbers are such identifiers. In future, more digital identifiers would be supported.

Perform the following tasks to begin your registration process on the Consent Manager app:

- Install the consent manager application on your phone.Download .apk file.
- Open the Consent Manager app and click on the Register button to register as a patient.
- You will be asked to enter your mobile phone number, in order to verify your identity. Make sure that you enter the mobile number which you generally provide at the hospitals that you visit.
- Enter your mobile number. Take care to enter valid phone number.
- Click the Verify button to authenticate your mobile number.
- You will receive an SMS from the ABDM Consent Manager, along with an OTP (One Time Password).
- Enter the OTP in the Consent Manager app.
- If your OTP is validated, you will be directed to a Registration screen. If your OTP is not validated, you will be prompted to enter a valid OTP

#### Create Consent Manager ID And Password

Perform the following tasks to create your Consent Manager Id and password:

- After successful verification of your mobile number, you will be taken through a simple process to create your profile, and also your username and password.
- You may choose to register using Aadhaar, which will seek Aadhaar verification from you, and once verified your basic profile will be prefilled. Note, ABDM Consent Manager does not store your Aadhaar number or Virtual ID.
- Type a username, using only letters and numbers. You can use the special characters (.) and (-). Your username should be of minimum of 4 characters and maximum of 150 characters.
- Type a password, of at least 8 characters, containing one uppercase and one lowercase letter, and one special character and one number. Memorize the password.
- If you decided to proceed without Aadhaar, you will have to provide basic details. Provide your name, which will be used to locate your account in hospitals. This field is mandatory.
- Select a gender. This field is also mandatory.
- Select your year of birth. Hospitals will use this information to filter patient accounts.
- Click the Register link after entering all the required information. If the username you've entered has already been taken, you'll be asked to create a different username.
- If the username you've entered is accepted, you will be directed to a screen where you can search for hospitals to which you wish to link your account.

### Discovery Of Patient Information

When a consent manager presents a set of verifiable Ids to an HIP system and requests for a particular patient’s records, the process of information discovery begins. Upon receipt of the request, the HIP health repository reverts with a set of care context labels (in masked form). The following flow diagram details the flows that take place during patient information discovery:

Flow Diagram - Discovery

### Linking To HIP Health Repositories

This flow begins once a patient initiates via consent manager with a request to the HIP to link the care context labels to the patient’s Consent Manager’s User ID. To enable the linkin, the HIP system provides a link reference number along with the proposed authentication type and its associated parameters.

The HIP system sends an OTP to the patient’s phone number which is associated with the patient’s verifiable ID. The patient, via patient app, submits the OTP to the HIP system within the stipulated time, along with the link reference number. If the patient is successfully authenticated by the HIP, then the HIP sends out a success message to the patient, indicating that the linking is now complete. The following flow diagrams details the flows that take place while linking to a health repository representing an HIP:

Flow Diagram - Linking Initialization and Confirmation

### Consent Request From an HIU to Consent Manager

Obtaining the patient’s consent for information sharing begins when the HIU system issues a consent request to the consent manager for the patient’s data. The CM acknowledges the request and sends back a consent request ID to the HIU. The CM notifies the patient that a HIU has made a consent request. The patient can view the request details, and choose to either grant consent or deny it.

Subsequently, the CM notifies the HIU requester of the patient’s consent or denial status.

- If the request is granted, the CM sends across the Ids of the consent artefacts that were created against the request, to the HIU.
- If the request is denied, the CM notifies the HIU of the denial; in this case, no consent artefacts are transferred from the CM to the HIU.

The following flow diagrams detail the flows that take place when patient provides consent to the HIU which wants to access his or her data:

Flow Diagram - Consent Request by HIU

Flow Diagram - Consent Notification from CM to HIU

Flow Diagram - Consent Notification from CM to HIP

#### Fetching a Consent Artefact From a CM

Once the patient grants consent to the HIU, the CM notifies the HIU system of the consent grant. Now the HIU system can go ahead and issue a request to the CM for each consent artefact listed in the request, as illustrated in the following figure:

Fetching Consent Artefact

### Data Request and Transfer

The data request and transfer process between the HIU, CM and HIP passes through the following three stages:

#### First Stage

- The HIU requester sends out a request for a patient’s health information to the HIP, through the CM.
- The CM sets a transaction ID (txn-id) for the entire data flow and communicates this Id to the health repositories of the HIU and the HIP.
- The HIU’s health repository embeds three key elements within the health information request:The consent ID corresponding to the consent artefact against which the information request is being made.A data push URL, which is a callback URL that indicators where the information can be pushed by the HIP’s health repository. This URL can be different from the HIU’s access URL, provided at the time of registration with the gateway. The HIU can specify a different URL for the data flow, in order to keep its identity secret to the extent possible.Several parameters such as the date-time range for the requested and a set of encryption parameters for the HIP repository to encrypt the information. The Elliptic-curve Diffie–Hellman based encryption standard is used for encrypting health information.

The HIU’s health repository relays all this information to the CM through the gateway. From the CM, the information is relayed to the HIP’s health repository (via the gateway).

#### Second Stage

Once the HIP repository receives the information, it first validates the information request, as follows:

- The HIP finds out if the consent ID corresponds to an expired, paused or revoked artefact.
- It then checks if the request’s date-time range will correspond to the range for which the consent artefact allows information access. It also ensures that the encryption parameters are correctly defined.
- Once the above checks are made and validated, the HIP health repository encrypts the requested health records and forwards it along with the transaction ID to the HIU’s data push URL, after signing the encrypted data with its long-term private key.

#### Third Stage

Finally, the CM receives notifications from both the HIP and the HIU. The HIP’s health repository notifies the CM that the requested information was transmitted to the HIU. The HIU’s health repository sends a notification that the requested information was successfully received, or that the request failed.

