### FHIR Validator

As HIP, before you encrypt and send over the health record document (as FHIR document) to the Sandbox HIU, it is advised that you validate the data using the FHIR validator. You can download the FHIR validator from the link provided below, which also has explanation of how to validate a FHIR resource.

- Reference FHIR resource validator (Java)
- Online FHIR resource validator

For example, if you have downloaded the FHIR validator jar already, you can validate your record by using the following command

```
java -jar org.hl7.fhir.validator.jar example-prescription-record.json -version 4.0.1
```

### FHIR Client Libraries

Refer to the following sections for reference client libraries and open source implementations:

- Reference implementations- check out the reference implementation section.
- Open source implementations- provides other libraries written in Delphi, PHP, Python, JavaScript etc

### Encryption Libraries

Here is a sample of the set of libraries that you can use:

- Bouncy castle (ensure the X dhpk is a big integer)
- Openssl
- NaCl
- Libgcrypt
- PyNaCl
- TweetNaCl

While most libraries allow exporting of public keys, these keys are compressed and encoded for the most part. It is best to use the libraries to extract the uncompressed form of the public key, that will start with a hex 04. The 32 bytes after the 04 is the X.

The ECDH mechanism ensures that the shared key dh(U,P) can also be computed at the other end by the HIU using the values dhpk(P) (HIP's ECDH public key) and dhsk(U) (HIU's ECDH private key). For this reason, the HIP must ensure that the encrypted data is sent along with dhpk(P) and rand(P).

Encryption and Decryption documentation has detailed guide and examples.


