## Building HIP

API Documentation for building the HIP is available here:

HIP APIs

Link to HIP Document

The flows which have to be built are:

### 1. Discovery Of The Patient’s Information At The HIP

When the gateway calls an HIP system and requests for a particular patient’s records with a set of verifiable Ids, the process of information discovery begins. Upon receipt of the request, the HIP health repository reverts with a set of care context labels (in masked form). The following flow diagram details the flows that take place during patient information discovery from the HIP perspective

Discovery

### 2. Verify The Patient And Link The Carecontext As Requested By The Patient

This flow begins once a patient initiates a link request to the HIP to link the care context to the patient’s Consent Manager’s User ID. To enable the linking, the HIP system returns a link reference number along with the authentication type and its associated parameters.

The HIP system sends an OTP to the patient’s phone number. Note, the phone number for OTP communication from HIP may be the same as verified by the CM or maybe a different number that the patient has chosen as preferred mode of communication with HIP - meaning it's up to the HIP to choose the phone number it sends OTP to. The patient, via patient app, submits the OTP received from the HIP system within the stipulated time. If the patient is successfully authenticated by the HIP, the linking is now complete. The following flow diagrams details the flows that take place while linking to a health repository representing an HIP

Linking

### 3. Consent Request Notification From An HIU To Consent Manager

Obtaining the patient’s consent for information sharing begins when the HIU system issues a consent request to the consent manager for the patient’s data. The patient can view the request details, and choose to either grant consent or deny it.

The CM, notifies the HIU, and also sends a notification to the HIP of the patient’s consent if granted.

The following flow diagrams detail the flows that take place when patient provides consent and the notification that is sent to the HIP

Consent Notification from CM to HIP

### 4. Data Request And Transfer

The data request and transfer process between the HIU, CM and HIP passes through the following three stages:

#### First Stage

- The HIU system for patient’s health information to the HIP, through the CM against a valid granted consent.
- The CM assigns a transaction ID for the entire data flow and communicates this Id to the health repositories of the HIU and the HIP.
- The HIU’s health repository embeds three key elements within the health information request:The consent ID corresponding to the consent artefact against which the information request is being made.A data push URL, which is a callback URL that indicators where the information can be pushed by the HIP’s health repository. This URL can be different from the HIU’s access URL, provided at the time of registration with the gateway. The HIU can specify a different URL for the data flow, in order to keep its identity secret to the extent possible.Several parameters such as the date-time range for the requested and a set of encryption parameters for the HIP repository to encrypt the information. The Elliptic-curve Diffie–Hellman based encryption standard is used for encrypting health information.

The HIU’s health repository relays all this information to the CM through the gateway. From the CM, the information is relayed to the HIP’s health repository (via the gateway).

#### Second Stage

Once the HIP repository receives the information, it first validates the information request, as follows:

- The HIP finds out if the consent ID corresponds to an expired, paused or revoked artefact.
- It then checks if the request’s date-time range will correspond to the range for which the consent artefact allows information access. It also ensures that the encryption parameters are correctly defined.
- Once the above checks are made and validated, the HIP health repository encrypts the requested health records and forwards it along with the transaction ID to the HIU’s data push URL, after signing the encrypted data with its long-term private key.

#### Third Stage

Finally, the CM receives notifications from both the HIP and the HIU. The HIP’s health repository notifies the CM that the requested information was transmitted to the HIU. The HIU’s health repository sends a notification that the requested information was successfully received, or that the request failed.

All above 3 stages that pertains to HIP are shown in the following diagram:

Health Data Request and Transfer

### 5. Share Patient Profile With HIP

All ABDM compliant PHR Apps should provide the end-user with a functionality to scan a QR Code placed at the health facility counter using the phone’s camera or any other scanner and also from within the app thereby sharing his/her profile details with the ABDM compliant Health facility (HIP) and complete the express registration.

Share Patient Profile

This feature has now been enhanced further where post user scanning any counter agnostic QR code within the ABDM compliant health facility, in addition to user sharing of profile for registration, he/she will also be issued a token number which will be sent as notification to end user through ABDM complaint PHR applications installed on their smartphone.

This will help in streamlining the flow of patients through hospitals OPD’s and clinics and also alleviate the challenges and aftermaths that patients suffer from improper queue management.

#### Counter Agnostic QR Code Implementation:

User Journey

- Patient X is a pregnant female and arrives at the ABDM compliant health facility to get an appointment with a doctor in gynaecology department and does not have energies to stand in the queue while she waits for an OPD registration.
- The patient notices that the hospital has an ABDM compliant HIP (Health facility) QR code displayed. The same QR code is also placed everywhere within a given facility
- Patient ‘X’ scans the QR code on her smartphone.
- After scanning the QR code, she sees a pop-over list of all the PHR apps installed in his phone. She may select any application from this list and opens that application.
- Once the application selected by her is launched, she will be redirected to the share profile page.
- Once she provides the consent and clicks on “SHARE”and with this, she will complete the express registration process along with sharing her registration details with the ABDM compliant health facility. In addition to this she has also provided her consent to HIP to link her health records with the ABHA Address shared along with the profile details.
- Post this she is also notified about the token number in her respective PHR App.
- The patient can now wait in the waiting area of the hospital premises until her token number appears on the display screen of the respective OPD registration counter. If she misses her token number for any reasons, she can still go a skip counter meant specifically for these types of issues.
- Basis the token number she is directed to the counter where she is asked the follow up questions related to gynae and is accordingly allocated gynecology department along with issuance of OP slip

Use Cases

When a user scans the Health Facility QR code at the facility counter using the phone’s camera, the following use cases may trigger:

#### Use Case 1: ABDM Compliant PHR Application Is Already Installed In User’s Phone

After scanning the QR code, the user sees a pop-over list of all the PHR apps installed in his phone. He may select any application from this list and opens that application.

a. User is not logged-on in the PHR app : User is not logged-on in the PHR app: Once the user selected application launches, he/she will be redirected to the login page. After the successful login, he/she will be redirected to the share profile page.

For instance, here ABHA SBX app is already installed in the device. When the user scans the QR code with using any 3rd party camera or scanner, the flow is as below:

#### b. User is already logged-on in the PHR app: Once the user selected application launches, user will be redirected to the share profile page.

#### Use Case 2: No ABDM Compliant PHR Application Is Installed

After scanning the QR code, the user sees a browser from where he/she would be redirected to a User Initiated Deep link web page. This webpage will show the list of all the ABDM compliant PHR applications, from where the user has an option to select any application of his choice. The user would then be redirected to the respective application’s play store or Appstore page to install the app. Once the app is installed, the user may register/login and then would be redirected to the share profile page.

For instance, when user has no application installed on his phone, the flow will be as below:

#### Responsibilities of the PHR Application

- Display The Information That Will Be Shared To The User: All the information that will be shared with the HIP like Name, Date of Birth, Gender, Address, PHR Address, ABHA No, etc.
- Display Token Number
- Take Consent From The User For Sharing The Details: The PHR app must present the following consent language

You consent to the above information to be shared with<HIP Name>. They can use this information for your registration and linking your health records.

#### Responsibilities of The HMIS :

- Integration of queue management system with HMIS
- Realtime capturing of shared profile details in HMIS
- Dynamic queue management for offline and Online (QR code) patients
- Skip Queue management
- Missing Number Handling
- ABHA System Integration
- Linking of existing UHID with ABHA system for follow-up patients and auto assignment of new UHID and linking the same with ABHA system for new patients
- Configurable time duration for token expiry in API as per HSPA/HIP/HMIS requirements, to stop the patient from scanning the QR code multiple times post issuance of token
- Assigning unique and in-sequence token number to each patient
- To put a check that scans can only be done during the OPD timings (weekend/weekday) of any facility
- Patient to get the token only for the current day, provision to display date/validity of token.

#### Points For Consideration For Integrators

Patient information mapped to ABHA will be visible across all the counters that are integrated with the Queue management system (to be installed and managed by the HIP). Patient profile will be shared among all the counters and dynamic logic would be used for mapping the QR code to the counter. For supporting above logic, the counter number should be optional in payload for both QR code generation and scanning.

